WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.680
Every last part of a major motion picture
is carefully planned for years by a crew that

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.290
numbers in the hundreds. The lighting has
to be just right, the camera angles precisely

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:17.350
mapped out, and the lines memorized by the
actors. But sometimes magic still happens

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.849
spontaneously. A last minute or spur-of-the-moment
acting choice is so clever that it stays in

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:26.019
the movie. Those moments are often the most
memorable parts of a film. We've covered some

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:30.720
of the most amazing unplanned cinematic situations
in our video, 10 Spectacular Movie Scenes

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.829
That Were Improvised. Now, here are nine more
times the actors went off-book.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:36.700
The Silence of the Lambs

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.410
It's already a terrifying moment in a terrifying
movie. Hannibal Lecter coldly admits to Clarice

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:45.130
Starling that he killed and ate part of a
census taker. He doesn't really need to do

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.890
anything demonstrably monstrous, and the script
didn't call for it. But actor Anthony Hopkins

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.540
reportedly wanted to genuinely spook his co-star,
Jodie Foster, not to mention the audience.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.330
So he came up with his little snake-like hiss:

00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:02.130
"I ate his liver with some fava beans and
a nice chianti."

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.320
(Hisses)

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:08.460
Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.230
All of the fight scenes in the first Indiana
Jones movie took weeks to choreograph—including

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:17.440
a lengthy sword vs. whip battle. But when
the time came to film it, Harrison Ford backed

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.050
out. He’d come down with food poisoning
the night before shooting. He simply couldn’t

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.200
go through with the elaborate physicality
of the scene. So, he ran an idea past director

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.360
Steven Spielberg that became one of the Indiana
Jones series' most famous moments. Instead

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.040
of fighting off a swordsman with his whip
in a few minutes, Indy just shoots him.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.060
End of fight. End of scene. And Ford was free
to go.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.369
Good Will Hunting

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:51.960
As Robin Williams' character tells a story
about his deceased wife waking herself up

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.229
with her own sleep farts, Matt Damon can't
stop laughing.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:58.590
"She used to fart in her sleep."

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:04.860
"Sorry I shared that with you. One night it
was so loud it woke the dog up."

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.520
The laughs are genuine, because Williams,
a gifted improviser, made up the whole story.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.060
Both the hilarious bit about the farting—and
then the really sad part about how intimate

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.360
moments like these are what defined a happy
marriage.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.780
"How do you like dem apples?"

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:20.670
Dumb and Dumber

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.160
The script for this Farrelly brothers comedy
called for Harry and Lloyd to get on a hitman's

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.510
nerves by arguing about jelly beans. The comedically
agile Jim Carrey and theatrically trained

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.780
Jeff Daniels thought the dialogue was weak.
So they tried out several ideas, eventually

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:33.950
deciding on...

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.330
"Want to hear the most annoying sound in the
world?"

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.260
(Yells)
"Guys! Guys! Guys!"

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.980
The Godfather

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:48.569
The scene was supposed to be brutal, but simple.
High-ranking mobster Peter Clemenza, played

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:52.250
by Richard Castellano, is on his way to a
hit with an underling when his wife asks him

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.440
to pick up some cannoli. As scripted, Clemenza
and his stooge kill the guy, and Clemenza

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.290
utters "leave the gun." But when Castellano
filmed the scene, he remembered the other

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.890
thing his character was supposed to do. After
the hit, he quipped:

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.430
"Take the cannoli."

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.530
...instructing the underling to grab the box
of cannoli on the murdered man's dashboard.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:10.810
Delicious.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.310
Zoolander

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.670
Truly great moments of improv work when they
fit in perfectly within the movie's themes.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.459
Case in point: Derek Zoolander asks an important
question to David Duchovny’s hand model

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:21.050
character:

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.810
"So why male models?"

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.959
Duchovny delivers a long monologue about how
male models are involved in an assassination

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:31.569
plot. But afterward, Ben Stiller had reportedly
forgotten his next line...so he asks the same

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:35.250
question he'd asked moments before, leading
to one of the greatest improvised responses

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.400
in comedy history:

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.090
"But why male models?"

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.260
"You serious? I just told you that a moment
ago."

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.260
"Right."

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:50.370
Pretty Woman

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.300
Richard Gere’s character Edward, was supposed
to present Vivian, played by Julia Roberts

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.459
with a fancy necklace. While filming, Gere
decided to prank his co-star, and quickly

00:03:58.459 --> 00:03:59.559
shut the box.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.220
(Laughs)

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.489
Roberts was taken aback, and her laughter
and surprise were so genuine (and charming)

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:08.209
that the take stayed in the movie.

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:09.430
The Warriors

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.790
As the Warriors nemesis Luther, actor David
Patrick Kelly was only supposed to ominously

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:18.780
bang a bunch of bottles together. Instead,
he added a little something extra:

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:25.470
"Warriors...come out to play!"

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:32.160
"Warriors...come out to play!"

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.410
A schoolyard taunt wasn't in the script, but
it so perfectly built up the drama for the

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.669
final confrontation that director Walter Hill
let it stand. It's now far and away the most

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:43.900
famous line from the 1979 cult film.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:44.770
Aliens

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.970
After a massive attack from the aliens kills
a whole bunch of humans, things look pretty

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.389
bleak. The line was written as, "That's it.
What are we going to do now?" But actor Bill

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:57.540
Paxton was just feeling the vibe of Private
Hudson, his mouthy and eccentric character.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:01.290
As a result, he ad-libbed the lines he'll
probably be best remembered for until the

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.010
end of time:

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.060
"That's it man. Game over, man. Game over!"

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.270
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.659
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which improvised scene was your favorite...

